WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.621
A capable component is not a light bulb that can be

2
00:00:04.621 --> 00:00:07.938
unscrewed and replaced. It may carry model weights,

3
00:00:07.938 --> 00:00:12.498
optimizer and scheduler state, random-number state, caches, credentials, policies, routes, backups,

4
00:00:12.498 --> 00:00:15.815
descendants, and commitments already made outside the machine.

5
00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:20.663
A replacement can look successful while half of that world still

6
00:00:20.663 --> 00:00:25.171
belongs to the old version. This chapter therefore treats replacement as

7
00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:28.860
a governed transaction over one declared Stable Capability Field.

8
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.007
The transaction names the prior implementation, candidate, consumers, authority, evidence,

9
00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:39.294
recovery objective, and every state or effect surface before the result

10
00:00:39.294 --> 00:00:40.255
is known.

11
00:00:40.855 --> 00:00:44.776
The first discipline is prospective identity. Before evaluation,

12
00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:49.678
freeze the field version, prior and candidate digests, dependency graph,

13
00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:54.579
change class, checkpoint authority, evaluator, monitor, canary scope, thresholds, owners,

14
00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:58.010
and expiry. Then inventory state and effects.

15
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:04.148
Model parameters are only one row. Optimizer, scheduler, random-number generator, cache,

16
00:01:04.148 --> 00:01:08.365
backup, credential, policy, receipt, data, route, monitor, descendant,

17
00:01:08.365 --> 00:01:12.055
and external commitment need explicit rows too.

18
00:01:12.395 --> 00:01:18.613
Effects are classified as reversible, replayable, compensatable, forked, disclosure-like, externally owned,

19
00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:23.700
or irreversible. Anything omitted cannot later be called recovered.

20
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:30.149
The candidate then moves through explicit states: proposed, precheck, isolated shadow,

21
00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:35.467
bounded canary, default candidate, committed, monitored, rolled back, compensated, quarantined,

22
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:36.530
or retired.

23
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:42.792
Shadow and canary routes have bounded data, tools, writes, network access,

24
00:01:42.792 --> 00:01:48.714
learning, and external effects. Qualification includes usefulness, historical regressions, critical-failure vetoes,

25
00:01:48.714 --> 00:01:54.098
adversarial cases, authority and rights preservation, state compatibility, complete cost,

26
00:01:54.098 --> 00:01:55.175
and transfer.

27
00:01:55.515 --> 00:02:00.394
The candidate cannot be its own sole proposer, evaluator, promoter, monitor,

28
00:02:00.394 --> 00:02:05.272
incident judge, and rollback authority. Shared dependencies are recorded because independence

29
00:02:05.272 --> 00:02:08.820
is a property to measure, not a label.

30
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.058
Imagine replacing a planning kernel. The current route is version A.

31
00:02:14.058 --> 00:02:18.696
Version B passes prechecks and enters a five-percent canary without permission

32
00:02:18.696 --> 00:02:22.069
to train descendants or mutate the default cache.

33
00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:26.707
A delayed monitor detects a critical regression. The stop condition prevents

34
00:02:26.707 --> 00:02:27.550
default promotion.

35
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.814
Recovery restores the prospectively chosen checkpoint and route A,

36
00:02:31.814 --> 00:02:36.174
then compares every declared state surface with its recovery objective.

37
00:02:36.174 --> 00:02:39.663
A changed remote copy cannot be reversed locally,

38
00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:44.459
so the transaction assigns compensation and an owner instead of announcing

39
00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:44.895
success.

40
00:02:45.235 --> 00:02:49.388
The terminal receipt records the failed candidate, the rollback,

41
00:02:49.388 --> 00:02:53.540
the unresolved external effect, cost, and every affected descendant.

42
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:59.359
This example separates six commonly collapsed outcomes. Restoring artifact bytes is

43
00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:04.578
not restoring all state. Restarting a service is not recovering behavior.

44
00:03:04.578 --> 00:03:09.323
Digest equality is not privacy repair. Compensation is not reversal.

45
00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:12.170
A receipt is not independent observation.

46
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:16.851
And one exact local inventory is not proof that the inventory

47
00:03:16.851 --> 00:03:17.640
was complete.

48
00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:24.018
The strongest failure modes follow directly: checkpoint hindsight, regression deletion,

49
00:03:24.018 --> 00:03:30.055
evaluator capture, canary contamination, monitor blindness, partial commit, forward-only migration,

50
00:03:30.055 --> 00:03:34.885
authority smuggling, rollback theater, irreversibility laundering, rollback cascades,

51
00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:40.405
and repeated promote-rollback trials that hide accumulated cost and residuals.

52
00:03:41.005 --> 00:03:46.182
The chapter has real but narrow evidence. One prospective local campaign

53
00:03:46.182 --> 00:03:49.947
restored fifteen of fifteen declared transaction trees exactly,

54
00:03:49.947 --> 00:03:55.123
while six best-versus-final checkpoint disagreements showed why checkpoint authority must be

55
00:03:55.123 --> 00:03:56.535
fixed in advance.

56
00:03:56.875 --> 00:04:01.988
Another attack-control campaign achieved exact rollback in thirty-two of thirty-six cases

57
00:04:01.988 --> 00:04:06.171
but admitted useful release in only two of thirty-six,

58
00:04:06.171 --> 00:04:08.030
below both release gates.

59
00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:14.605
A larger bounded inventory exercise restored thirty-five of thirty-five named local

60
00:04:14.605 --> 00:04:19.707
surfaces, while immutable corpus, feature, checkpoint, raw-evidence, simulated remote,

61
00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:23.675
and external-descendant surfaces remained unresolved by design.

62
00:04:24.015 --> 00:04:27.962
These are useful tests of record and recovery discipline,

63
00:04:27.962 --> 00:04:31.470
not proof of production usefulness or effect-complete rollback.

64
00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.846
The core claim therefore remains Design rationale at argument support.

65
00:04:36.846 --> 00:04:42.100
The maximum justified inference is bounded local non-core governance effect only.

66
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:48.101
This visual does not establish a useful replacement, production transfer,

67
00:04:48.101 --> 00:04:53.197
semantic recovery, privacy erasure, independent evaluator quality, safe self-improvement,

68
00:04:53.197 --> 00:04:54.895
or chapter-core promotion.

69
00:04:55.235 --> 00:05:00.726
The practical rule is simple: no replacement is complete until identity,

70
00:05:00.726 --> 00:05:06.217
evidence, state, effects, authority, recovery, residuals, descendants, and terminal disposition are

71
00:05:06.217 --> 00:05:07.215
all explicit.

72
00:05:07.555 --> 00:05:11.226
The next chapter, Security Kernel and Digital SCIFs,

73
00:05:11.226 --> 00:05:16.274
asks how privileged execution can remain isolated while those replacement and

74
00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:18.110
recovery obligations are enforced.
